stence-universally-closed}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0002": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0003": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0004": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0005": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0006": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:a8843faa5d25aa2475efdcda20d92f4b930fd62dd07fd1510ec4254f8d547262", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:fd9c5367dbb8d5b70d41bdb93c07f1292b168e18291ba81af22f787cfd3de778", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:4e3230d12d14c70593675c2023522ae8cee039d8155fb7d6d7962ffcd6b9ed0f", "source_text": "We are going to write out a detailed proof, but we strongly urge the\nreader to find their own proof, inspired by reading the argument\ngiven in the proof of Lemma <REF_0001>.\n\n<STRUCT_0001><STRUCT_0002>\nAssume <MATH_0001> is quasi-separated and for every diagram\n(<REF_0002>) and choice of <MATH_0002> as in\nDefinition <REF_0003>\nthe category of dotted arrows is a setoid.\nBy Lemma <REF_0004> it suffices to show that\n<MATH_0003>\nis a closed immersion. By\nLemma <REF_0005>\nit in fact suffices to show that <MATH_0004> is\nuniversally closed.\nEither of these lemmas tells us that <MATH_0005> is quasi-compact\nby our assumption that <MATH_0006> is quasi-separated.\n\n<STRUCT_0003><STRUCT_0004>\nIn this paragraph we will show that <MATH_0007> satisfies the existence\npart of the valuative criterion. Consider a <MATH_0008>-commutative solid diagram\n<MATH_0009>\nand let <MATH_0010> be any <MATH_0011>-morphism\nwitnessing the <MATH_0012>-commutativity of the diagram (we use <MATH_0013> instead\nof the letter <MATH_0014> used in Definition <REF_0006>).\nNote that <MATH_0015>; we will use this below.\nObserve that <MATH_0016> and\n<MATH_0017>.\nThus we see that <MATH_0018> is a <MATH_0019>-arrow <MATH_0020>\ncompatible with <MATH_0021> and <MATH_0022>.\nSet <MATH_0023> and <MATH_0024>.\nReading the arguments given in the proof of\nLemma <REF_0007>\nbackwards, we see that <MATH_0025> is an automorphism of the\ndotted arrow <MATH_0026> with\n<MATHSEG_0001>equal to<MATHSEG_0002>\nOn the other hand, <MATH_0027> is an automorphism too, hence\nwe conclude <MATH_0028> from the assumption on <MATH_0029>.\nThen we can take as dotted arrow for the displayed diagram above\nthe morphism <MATH_0030> with <MATH_0031>-morphisms\n<MATH_0032> given by <MATH_0033>\nand <MATH_0034> given by <MATH_0035>.\n\n<STRUCT_0005><STRUCT_0006>\nBy Lemma <REF_0008> any base change of <MATH_0036>\nsatisfies the existence part of the valuative criterion.\nSince <MATH_0037> is representable by algebraic spaces, it suffices to\nshow that <MATH_0038> is universally closed after a base change\nby a morphism <MATH_0039>\nwhich is surjective and smooth and with <MATH_0040> an algebraic space\n(see Properties of Stacks, Section\n<REF_0009>).\nThis base change <MATH_0041> is a quasi-compact\nmorphism of algebraic spaces which\nsatisfies the existence part of the valuative criterion\nand hence is universally closed by\nMorphisms of Spaces, Lemma\n<REF_0010>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:c8858ec2d514f4571cd06a83d59069c45c6c13fac1ea8676e2bb93cab5c7658e", "unit_id": "tag:0CLR:proof"}
